arkets price his win odds near ninety percent. The farm-labor controversy and those closer polls keep this at Likely rather than Solid Republican.", "watch": "If independent pollsters confirm the single-digit margins shown in Democratic-sponsored surveys, the race could move toward genuine tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's governor's race pits incumbent Republican Jim Pillen against Democratic challenger Lynne Walz in a state that leans ten points Republican. Most professional raters treat Pillen as a strong favorite: five of seven call the race Solid Republican, and prediction markets put his win odds near ninety percent. Neutral pollsters in mid-September showed Pillen ahead by eight and seventeen points among likely voters.\n\nThe wrinkle is a pair of Democratic-sponsored surveys, one showing a tie at forty percent and another a one-point Pillen edge, following a New York Times report about undocumented workers at Pillen's farm operation. Those surveys likely flatter Walz, but the tightening trend is real enough to keep this from being a lock. Pillen's incumbency and the state's partisan lean still make him clearly favored; Walz needs the race to keep moving her way through the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0585, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0639, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0714, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0694, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0605, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0982, "margin": -8.5}]}